@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:architecture] skate-resources-are-exported-bytes
The skating Session MUST construct its complete simulation resources from the exported simulation package bytes.
It MUST NOT read an original animation archive, authoring JSON, private asset root, socket or subprocess.
Clip tracks and metadata MUST retain binary32 words, bank identity, source offsets and graph binding order.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] skate-one-fixed-tick
Session::tick MUST advance exactly one native simulation tick from one raw controller packet. Marker hold time MUST
advance by that simulation period, not render-frame time. The explicit host TRANSFER bit MUST NOT consume the
native LB modifier used for marker placement and return.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] skate-stow-keeps-native-authority
Normal stow MUST retain skating ownership until the native rider has remained on-foot and grounded for 0.2 seconds.
A V toggle before that handback MUST cancel the pending stow: it requests a mount when already on foot,
and stops further dismount requests when still riding. An already accepted native animation may finish.
Neither action may invent a physical state.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] skate-retains-rail-topology
Session construction and collision replacement MUST preserve authored grind-line IDs, point order and closure.
Only the spline adapter MAY add a last-to-first chord for a closed line that does not already repeat its endpoint.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] skate-hud-observes-native-results
Trick announcements and their start stance MUST come from the announced native scoring carrier. Pump labels MUST
count only the native intentional ground pump gain, not coasting, pushes or boost-zone speed. Spin labels MUST measure
the rider root, not a board shove-it. Retained telemetry MUST advance on simulation ticks without changing score or physics.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:correctness] skate-local-bail-recovery
Local bail recovery MUST preserve the displayed physical body's location and momentum, settle on the actual world
surface, and rise continuously there. A successful get-up MUST NOT request or pass through the historical checkpoint
Teleporting path. The existing board/rider assembly remains the only solver; a far or overturned board changes its
location only while hidden. Intermediate get-up collision shapes MUST retain the lower endpoint world height and
terrain clearance, including on slopes. Native graphs own the standing target; the host MUST NOT force a clip.
